2. Overall Description
2.1 Product Perspective
The project is a standalone, notebook-based data cleaning workflow. It is the first stage of a data analysis pipeline: its output is meant to feed later analysis and visualisation work.
2.2 Product Functions
•	Load the raw CSV file into a DataFrame.
•	Explore the data structure, types, summary statistics, missing values and duplicates.
•	Select rows and columns with loc and iloc.
•	Clean the data: fix text, convert types, handle impossible values, fill missing values, drop unneeded columns and remove duplicates.
•	Verify the result and save the cleaned data to a new CSV file.
2.3 User Characteristics
The user is a data analyst student with basic Python and pandas knowledge. No advanced statistics or programming skill is needed to run the notebook.
2.4 Operating Environment
•	Platform: Google Colab (browser based, no installation).
•	Language: Python 3.
•	Library: pandas.
•	Input and output format: CSV.
2.5 Constraints
•	Only pandas is used for the cleaning. No other data cleaning library is allowed.
•	Only commands taught in the course are used.
•	Colab cannot read files from a local drive such as C:\, so the file is uploaded with files.upload() or read from Google Drive.
•	Files uploaded to Colab are deleted when the runtime resets.
2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies
•	The input file is a valid CSV with a header row.
•	The user has a Google account and an internet connection.
•	Column names in the input file do not change.
3. Input Data Description
The raw file employee_data_messy.csv has 103 rows and 9 columns.
•	Emp_ID: Employee identifier (integer).
•	Name: Employee name (text).
•	Age: Age in years (numeric).
•	City: City of work (text).
•	Department: Department name (text).
•	Salary: Salary (text in the raw file).
•	Email: Email address (text).
•	Join_Date: Joining date (text).
•	Rating: Performance rating (numeric).
3.1 Known Data Quality Problems
•	Missing values in Age (9), Salary (6), Email (5) and Rating (11).
•	Impossible ages: -5 and 250.
•	One impossible salary of 9,999,999 where all others are between 25,000 and 120,000.
•	Salary stored as text, for example Rs. 93000.
•	Inconsistent capital letters and extra spaces in Name, Department and City. For example HR and hr, or Mumbai, MUMBAI and Jaipur  (with a trailing space). City has 18 spellings for 8 real cities.
•	Mixed date formats in Join_Date.
•	3 fully duplicated rows.
4. Specific Requirements
4.1 Functional Requirements
Loading
•	FR-1: The system shall load the raw CSV file into a pandas DataFrame using pd.read_csv().
Exploration
•	FR-2: The system shall display sample rows using head(), tail() and sample().
•	FR-3: The system shall display the shape, column names and data types using shape, columns, dtypes and info().
•	FR-4: The system shall display summary statistics using describe() and describe(include='all').
•	FR-5: The system shall display category counts and unique values using value_counts() and unique().
•	FR-6: The system shall count missing values with isna().sum() and duplicate rows with duplicated().sum().
•	FR-7: The system shall select columns by name and rows with loc and iloc.
Cleaning
•	FR-8: The system shall drop the Rating and Join_Date columns using drop(columns=[...]).
•	FR-9: The system shall remove extra spaces and standardise capital letters in Name, Department and City using str.strip() and str.title(). It shall write HR and IT in capitals.
•	FR-10: The system shall convert Email to lower case.
•	FR-11: The system shall remove the Rs. prefix and commas from Salary and convert it to a number using pd.to_numeric().
•	FR-12: The system shall mark ages below 18 or above 65 as missing, and salaries above 1,000,000 as missing.
•	FR-13: The system shall fill missing Age and Salary with the column median using fillna().
•	FR-14: The system shall fill missing Email with the text not_available.
•	FR-15: The system shall remove duplicate rows with drop_duplicates(keep='first') and assign the result back to the DataFrame.
Verification and Output
•	FR-16: The system shall verify that no missing values and no duplicate rows remain.
•	FR-17: The system shall save the cleaned DataFrame to employee_data_clean.csv using to_csv(index=False).
4.2 Non-Functional Requirements
•	NFR-1 Usability: Each step shall be in its own notebook cell with a short comment, so a beginner can follow it.
•	NFR-2 Reproducibility: Running all cells in order on the same input shall always give the same output.
•	NFR-3 Performance: The whole notebook shall run in under one minute for a dataset of about 100 rows.
•	NFR-4 Portability: The notebook shall run in Google Colab with no installation.
•	NFR-5 Maintainability: Cleaning shall use only standard pandas methods so it is easy to read and extend.
•	NFR-6 Data safety: The original raw file shall not be changed. The cleaned data shall be saved as a separate file.
4.3 External Interface Requirements
•	User interface: Colab notebook cells and their printed output.
•	File interface: input employee_data_messy.csv, output employee_data_clean.csv, both in CSV format.
•	Software interface: Python 3 and pandas.
5. Output Specification
The cleaned file employee_data_clean.csv shall have:
•	100 rows and 7 columns: Emp_ID, Name, Age, City, Department, Salary, Email.
•	No missing values and no duplicate rows.
•	Five clean Department values: HR, Finance, Sales, IT and Marketing.
•	Eight clean City values.
•	Age between 22 and 58 and Salary between 25,000 and 120,000.
•	Salary as a numeric column.
6. Acceptance Criteria
•	df.isna().sum().sum() returns 0.
•	df.duplicated().sum() returns 0.
•	df.shape returns (100, 7).
•	df['Department'].nunique() returns 5 and df['City'].nunique() returns 8.
•	df['Salary'].dtype is numeric.
•	employee_data_clean.csv exists and opens with the expected columns.
7. Risks and Limitations
•	Filling with the median adds estimated values, not real ones. Many missing values would weaken later analysis.
•	Dropping Rating and Join_Date loses that information. Join_Date could be kept by converting it with pd.to_datetime().
•	The age range of 18 to 65 and the salary limit of 1,000,000 are chosen by the author and are assumptions about this dataset.
•	Uploaded files disappear when the Colab runtime resets, so the upload cell must be run again.
8. Future Enhancements
•	Convert Join_Date to a datetime column instead of dropping it.
•	Visualise the cleaned data with matplotlib or seaborn.
•	Add automatic checks that confirm the acceptance criteria.
•	Package the cleaning steps into a reusable function.
